data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already threw `InvalidOperationException` for synchronous LINQ by default, but you could opt in to sync-over-async (e.g., via `EnableSyncOverAsync()`). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** Synchronous query/save operations on the Cosmos DB provider are no longer supported at all.

Every call in your repository will throw `InvalidOperationException`:

| Method | Broken call |
|---|---|
| `GetAll()` | `ToList()` |
| `GetById()` | `First()` |
| `GetCount()` | `Count()` |
| `Save()` | `SaveChanges()` |

## Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` is purely in-memory (no I/O), so it stays synchronous — only the `SaveChanges` call needs the async counterpart.
- All callers up the chain must be updated to `await` these methods (async propagates "all the way up").
- The async LINQ extension methods (`ToListAsync`, `FirstAsync`, `CountAsync`) come from `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore`.
